## Coding Standards

### General Rules

- Use type hints/annotations on all function signatures
- Maximum function length: 50 lines (prefer <30)
- Maximum file length: 500 lines
- One class per file for major classes
- Prefer composition over inheritance

### Naming Conventions

| Element | Convention | Example |
|---------|------------|---------|
| Functions | snake_case (Python) / camelCase (TS) | `get_user_by_id` / `getUserById` |
| Classes | PascalCase | `UserService` |
| Constants | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | `MAX_RETRIES` |
| Files | snake_case (Python) / kebab-case (TS) | `user_service.py` / `user-service.ts` |
| Variables | snake_case / camelCase | `user_count` / `userCount` |

### Error Handling

- Never use bare `except:` or `catch` without type
- Always include context in error messages
- Log errors before re-raising when appropriate
- Use custom exception classes for domain errors

### Comments and Documentation

- Docstrings on all public functions/methods
- Comments explain WHY, not WHAT
- TODO format: `# TODO(username): description`
- Keep comments up to date with code

## Testing Requirements

- Minimum 80% code coverage for new code
- Unit tests for all business logic
- Integration tests for API endpoints
- Test file naming: `test_<module>.py` or `<module>.test.ts`

### Test Structure

```python
def test_<function>_<condition>_<expected_result>():
    # Arrange
    # Act
    # Assert
```

## Git Workflow

### Branch Naming

```
feature/<ticket>-<description>
fix/<ticket>-<description>
refactor/<description>
docs/<description>
```

### Commit Messages (Conventional Commits)

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>

<footer>
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `chore`

## Things to AVOID

- ❌ Do NOT modify files in `vendor/`, `node_modules/`, or `.venv/`
- ❌ Do NOT commit secrets, API keys, or credentials
- ❌ Do NOT use deprecated APIs (see `DEPRECATED.md` if exists)
- ❌ Do NOT bypass linter rules without documented reason
- ❌ Do NOT add dependencies without discussion
- ❌ Do NOT write code without tests

## Things to ALWAYS DO

- ✅ Read file before editing (verify current state)
- ✅ Make minimal, surgical changes
- ✅ Verify edits applied correctly
- ✅ Run relevant tests after changes
- ✅ Follow existing patterns in the codebase
- ✅ Ask for clarification if requirements are ambiguous

